--TEST--
`Core\Http\Response::bytes` hands back the reply's octets whole, including the ones no `string` could hold, while `::text` refuses that same body rather than repairing it — `rule:types/bytes`, `rule:errors/ambiguous-input-refused`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The claim is *the edge*: the body where the two readers of one slot stop
// agreeing. A reply carrying a NUL and a byte that begins no UTF-8 sequence is
// what a client fetching an image gets, and a transport that demanded text
// while reading the socket would have failed the call before the program said
// which reader it wanted.
//
// The octets are re-hexed rather than echoed, because they are not something
// an exact expectation can spell.
bytes $png = Core\Encoding::fromHex("89504e470d0a1a0a00ff");
Core\Test::answerHttp("https://cdn.example.com/logo.png", 200, {body: $png, headers: ["Content-Type" => "image/png"]});

var $image = Core\Http\Client::get("https://cdn.example.com/logo.png");
echo $image->status(), " ", $image->header("content-type") ?? "absent", "\n";
echo Core\Encoding::toHex($image->bytes()), "\n";
echo Core\Bytes::length($image->bytes()), "\n";

// The other half of the same body: `text` asks whether these octets are a
// `string` and answers that they are not, naming where they stop being text.
// Repairing them would hand the program a body the origin did not send and no
// way to tell.
try {
    echo $image->text(), "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $notText) {
    echo "text: refused\n";
}
--EXPECT--
200 image/png
89504e470d0a1a0a00ff
10
text: refused
